          usage: { ja: "「〜ときたら」は「〜は本当に困る／ひどすぎる／だめだ」などの気持ちで相手や物を非難するときに使われる。", en: "“〜ときたら” is used when criticizing a person or thing with feelings such as “〜 is really a pain / too awful / hopeless.”" },
          forms: ["[N] + ときたら"],
          examples: [
            { ja: "うちの子ときたら、いつもゲームばかりやっていて、声をかけても返事もしない。", en: "My kid, honestly — he's always playing games and doesn't even answer when I call him." },
            { ja: "まったくこのシャツときたら、いくつボタンがついているんだ。時間がないのに……。", en: "This shirt, honestly! How many buttons does it have? And I'm in a hurry…" },
            { ja: "最近の若い人ときたら、電車の中で床に座り込んだりして、恥ずかしくないのかしら。", en: "Young people these days — sitting on the floor of the train and so on. Aren't they ashamed?" },
          ],
          deepDive: "**Nときたら** picks out a topic — a person or thing close to the speaker — in order to **complain about it**: *as for ~, (honestly)…*. It works like は but adds an exasperated tone, and the rest of the sentence is a complaint (the usage note: 「〜は本当に困る／ひどすぎる／だめだ」).\n\n- Connection: noun + ときたら. Typical topics: family and colleagues (うちの子, 私の弟, 高橋君, トミーさん, あちらの担当者), things (このパソコン, このシャツ), groups (最近の若い人), even places and seasons (あの店, 今年の夏).\n- Register: conversational; the book marks it with the sweating-face icon (exasperation) and the casual-chat icon. It often comes with まったく, ほんとうに, もう: まったくこのシャツときたら….\n- The second half is a negative description, often exaggerated: いつも〜ばかり, 全然〜ない, 〜んだから, もう二度と〜ものか, 恥ずかしくないのかしら.\n\nThe topic is usually someone or something the speaker feels close to or responsible for — which is why it sounds like grumbling rather than a formal complaint. In the まとめ reading, the sister writes 私の弟ときたら今年40歳になるというのに… *that brother of mine — even though he turns 40 this year…*.\n\nCompare:\n- **〜といったら** (N2): *speaking of ~ (the first thing that comes to mind is…)*: 京都といったら寺だ — neutral or positive. ときたら is almost always critical.\n- **〜といったらない** (#83): *extremely ~, beyond words* — follows an adjective and intensifies a feeling; ときたら follows a noun and introduces a complaint about it. They can combine: うちの部長ときたら、しつこいといったらない.\n- **〜ったら** (casual topic marker): あの子ったら… — very similar in feel, even more colloquial.\n- **〜にかけては** (N2): *when it comes to ~ (he's the best)* — a positive evaluation of skill.\n- **Nときたら** also has a neutral *X naturally goes with Y* use (焼き肉ときたらビールだ), but the grammar point is the critical one.\n\nPitfall: don't use it in formal writing or about people you should show respect to in their presence. And the sentence must actually complain: ✗うちの子ときたら、成績がよくて自慢です.\n\nJLPT tip: if the sentence after the blank grumbles about someone's habits or a thing's defects, ときたら fits. In the listening, うちの会社ときたら、まったくもう〜 calls for a sympathetic question (え？どうしたの？), not congratulations.",
