          examples: [
            { ja: "どんなにひどいけがをしようが、アイスホッケーはやめられない。", en: "No matter how badly I get hurt, I can't give up ice hockey." },
            { ja: "{誰|だれ}が何と言おうが、一度決めたことを変えるわけにはいかないよ。", en: "Whatever anyone says, I can't change something I've already decided." },
            { ja: "お前がどこへ行こうと、{俺|おれ}の知ったことか。勝手にしろ！", en: "Wherever you go, what do I care? Do what you want!" },
            { ja: "医者に止められようが、たばこはやめられないよ。", en: "Even if the doctor tells me to stop, I can't quit smoking." },
            { ja: "雨が降ろうが風が吹こうが、犬の散歩は行かないわけにはいかないんです。", en: "Rain or wind, I have to take the dog for a walk." },
          ],
          deepDive: "**V-（よ）うが / V-（よ）うと** is a strong, somewhat literary *no matter (what / how) ~ / even if ~*. It uses the volitional form (しよう, 言おう, 行こう, 止められよう) + が or と, and the main clause states either **a determination that won't be shaken** (何年かかろうが、必ず新薬を開発します) or **a fact that can't be changed** (たとえ台風が上陸しようが、仕事を休むわけにはいかない) — the two uses in the usage note.\n\n- **With question words** (most common, as the book notes): 誰が何と言おうが, どこへ行こうと, 何があろうと, どんなにひどいけがをしようが, いくらお金を積もうが.\n- **With たとえ**: たとえ台風が上陸しようが.\n- **が and と** are interchangeable in this use.\n- Adjectives and nouns (see the clip note of #79): 高かろうが (い-adj → 〜かろう), 静かだろうが, 雨だろうと (な-adj/noun → だろう).\n- Two different verbs in a pair: 雨が降ろうが風が吹こうが (example ⑤: *rain or wind*).\n\nRegister: more emphatic than 〜ても; common in determined speech (the 部長: 相手が何を言おうが、気にしないで…宣伝しなきゃだめじゃないか *no matter what they say, you have to keep promoting our technology*) and in writing. Example ③ (お前がどこへ行こうと、俺の知ったことか *wherever you go, what do I care?*) shows it in rough speech too.\n\nCompare:\n- **〜ても / 〜たって**: neutral *even if*.\n- **〜としても** (N2): hypothetical concession, weaker.\n- **〜にかかわらず / 〜を問わず** (N2): *regardless of* — attach to nouns; factual and impersonal (年齢を問わず).\n- **V（よ）うがVまいが** (#79): *whether one ~s or not* — the same form with an affirmative/negative pair.\n- **〜であれ〜であれ** (#38): *be it A or B* — noun-based, formal.\n- **V（よ）うにもVない** (#84): the same volitional form but a different meaning: *want to ~ but can't*.\n\nPitfall: the main clause must hold **in spite of** the condition. ✗いくら頼もうが、手伝ってくれるだろう is odd because the result follows from the asking instead of resisting it. The practice paraphrases make this explicit: いくらお金を積もうが、人の心は買えない = お金を払っても人の心は絶対に買えない.\n\nJLPT tip: a question word or いくら / どんなに / たとえ + blank → 〜（よ）うが／〜（よ）うと. In the Check, 誰に反対されようが、私の決心は変わりません (*no matter who opposes me*) — the passive volitional 反対されよう is common.",
          see: [79, 38, 84],
          index: ["Vようが", "Vようと"],
